hermes v0.23.0: HERMES_SUDO_PROMPT=0 turns off every sudo password
prompt.

Symptom: the desktop app and the dashboard sometimes show a masked
"sudo password" card. hyperhermes agents run as unprivileged accounts without a sudo
password (NOPASSWD rules still work), so the card is a dead end, and
Dave never wants it.

Mechanism: tools/terminal_tool_sudo.py _transform_sudo_command asks for a
password when a command has a real `sudo`, no SUDO_PASSWORD is set,
nothing is cached, and HERMES_INTERACTIVE=1 or a sudo callback is
registered (tui_gateway/agent_callbacks.py, gateway/session_sudo_prompt.py
-> a `sudo` server request, hermes_cli/gateway_agent.py; else /dev/tty).
Bot Desktop's install button has a second card
(tools/bot_desktop/install.py _run -> display.install.sudo).

Why not SUDO_PASSWORD="" (config_defaults.py: "explicit empty string to
try empty without prompting"): any SUDO_PASSWORD in os.environ also
switches off the hardline guard against the model piping guessed
passwords into sudo's stdin mode (tools/approval_detection.py
_check_sudo_stdin_guard), and every sudo would become a real failed PAM
authentication.

With HERMES_SUDO_PROMPT=0 (hyperhermes sets it for every agent, see
../options.nix): no prompt on any surface, the command runs unchanged
(no stdin password) and fails with "sudo: a password is required"; the
failure tip (gateway and no-user contexts) says sudo is not available
instead of pointing at SUDO_PASSWORD; no "you will be prompted again" note; Bot Desktop's
install and `hermes computer-use` install print the command to run as
root, as on a host without sudo (a human at a terminal loses the getpass
prompt too). A configured SUDO_PASSWORD still applies.

